    "synthesis": {
      "model_calls": 1,
      "phase": "synthesis",
      "plan": {
        "A": "Use the measured-fastest radix candidate (aa19cdb376bf40ea; 18.49 ms) as the research starting point. Tune a general integer radix sort—such as pass width and histogram layout—while preserving full signed ordering, all input sizes, allocation safety, and the existing generation/checksum behavior. Verify output against the oracle; do not assume the recorded timing establishes correctness.",
        "B": "Investigate time outside the sort, especially whether the generated-case loop can be optimized while producing exactly the same values and preserving the RNG sequence and floating-point conversion semantics. Keep checksum and output behavior unchanged. The SIMD sortedness scan showed little measured benefit, so pursue it only if repeatable measurements justify it.",
        "C": "Explore parallelizing the radix approach rather than the slower measured parallel quicksort variants. For sufficiently large arrays, evaluate per-worker histograms and race-free prefix offsets/scatter with a serial fallback and a measured crossover threshold; cap parallelism at the available four cores. Preserve general integer ordering and verify correctness for every tested size.",
        "G": "Treat the radix candidate at 18.49 ms as the provisional best, not as a proven-correct final integration. Integrate tree outputs one at a time, validate each against the oracle, and measure the combined program on the same target and harness. Retain a combined change only when its own correctness and timing are confirmed; do not add individual gains arithmetically or carry over slower OpenMP quicksort changes without evidence.",
        "analysis": "The baseline is correct at 74.31 ms. The measured radix candidate is fastest at 18.49 ms (about 4.0x the baseline); the sampled parallel quicksort variants measured 32.91 and 36.16 ms, while other comparison-sort and OpenMP attempts were about 62–74 ms. This favors refining radix sorting, checking whether remaining runtime is outside the sort, and testing parallel radix cautiously on the four-core ARM64 target. Candidate correctness is not established by the supplied baseline correctness record, so require oracle validation before integration."
      },
